#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124173 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ae7e9c8-60e9-5f5e-afbf-1de6bb01bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Line to a Level Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "a01560be-9023-593a-87d3-7412f3b208a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3362" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y) = 6x^2+2y^2 - 2xy^2,$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (3,3).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y) = k$ at the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0 \\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y) = (3,3)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(12x-2{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+(4y-4xy)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇\\,f(3,3) & =(12(3)-2(3)^{2})i+(4(3)-4(3)(3))j \\\\  & =18\\,i-24\\,j \\\\  & =⟨18,-24⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the level curve that passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)=(3,3)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(3,3)\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-3⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨18,-24⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-3⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can leave our answer in this form. Or, if we want to leave our final answer in Cartesian form, we expand the dot product and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18(x-3)-24(y-3) & =0 \\\\ 18x-24y+18 & =0 \\\\ 3x-4y+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/assets/102f55fe0add794d3ee0f9f044b5e35c4403d4f160fe36e97d737d33ef3abb68.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124174 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "f6400ab7-3684-5f0d-b8f0-1c85b0b322ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-3363" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^2-3xy+4y=2.$ Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y) = x^2-3xy+4y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=2.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,1) = f_y(1,1) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(1,1) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3xy+4y)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3xy+4y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(-3x+4)\\text{ }i-(2x-3y)\\text{ }j \\\\ t(1,1) & =(-3(1)+4)\\text{ }i-(2(1)-3(1))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =1\\,i-(-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\mathbf i +\\mathbf j,$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070260/assets/0114bf524d085402bd27178d687a5faf176b7147c8e8726b84042b17823b6b0b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124175 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "c4ca2e8a-5a5b-54c6-983d-2f5e821084ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-3364" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $6xy+3y^2 =7+ y - 3x^2$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{j}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy+3{y}^{2} & =7+y-3{x}^{2} \\\\ 6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = 6xy+3y^2 - y + 3x^2 ,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=7.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x,y) = f_y(x,y) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(x,y) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2})\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6x+6y-1)\\,i-(6y+6x)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{j},$ we must have $\\mathbf t \\cdot \\mathbf j = 0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ((6x+6y-1)\\,i-(6y+6x)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -6y-6x & =0 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy+3{y}^{2} & =7+y-3{x}^{2} \\\\ 6x(-x)+3(-x)^{2} & =7+(-x)-3{x}^{2} \\\\ -6{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2} & =7-x-3{x}^{2} \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if $x=7,$ we get $y=-7$ and the corresponding point is $(7,-7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ae7e9c8-60e9-5f5e-afbf-1de6bb01bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Line to a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3392f2ea-7bd8-5a9c-8f75-bbcb4aee4fbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365953} {:answer/id #uuid "3068cec5-12bd-57f2-a69f-39dd20c8e0d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365954} {:answer/id #uuid "0a4e6769-3b61-54d8-a0ad-32e98aac3d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365955} {:answer/id #uuid "8a33f154-b3e9-53b6-88bb-18be76a03611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365956} {:answer/id #uuid "9583eddb-dae7-5dce-a4e5-debb3f397366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9583eddb-dae7-5dce-a4e5-debb3f397366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365957} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dd51780-056a-53c6-8aea-aacc889fc3cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c1bed00-c1da-539a-bd7b-b11b7bf684ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-130094" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-x+y,$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-x+y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-x+y)\\,j \\\\  & =({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }i+(2xy+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =((-1)^{2}-1)\\,i+(2(1)(-1)+1)\\,j \\\\  & =0\\,i-j \\\\  & =⟨0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-(-1)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ae7e9c8-60e9-5f5e-afbf-1de6bb01bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Line to a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72f2a4bb-cebc-55e1-9a75-437026044a5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365960} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9e1555-81a1-564b-9ea0-5488bed4dbfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365961} {:answer/id #uuid "0042bac8-ca7d-5d14-ad6a-ce18448d845d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365962} {:answer/id #uuid "c95e90dd-ff7e-5c00-8eaa-c5f4fbf695c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365963} {:answer/id #uuid "53224f6b-4759-5f49-acea-12618709253d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a9e1555-81a1-564b-9ea0-5488bed4dbfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365961} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f51af24-b51a-5d2e-abb6-cb06cae2df23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16e9a270-9782-5652-91b5-39a42de0d9ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-130096" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1),$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,0) & =\\frac{2(-1)}{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2(0)}{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\  & =(-1)i+0\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,0)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),\\,y-0⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ae7e9c8-60e9-5f5e-afbf-1de6bb01bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Line to a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7f1da5f-3838-5a70-81f0-c9ffb01370b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365967} {:answer/id #uuid "ceea421c-4915-5c85-9f0b-c65ea38fe9bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,4⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365968} {:answer/id #uuid "de25d71b-bea4-5750-a49c-b9bf16727cf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365969} {:answer/id #uuid "d7818237-311d-56e4-831f-ba844891720a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365970} {:answer/id #uuid "f97a590d-fce5-5e33-bb2e-45de846df8e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7818237-311d-56e4-831f-ba844891720a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365970} :answer-field/id #uuid "a523664d-f597-5f0d-bd78-2f5447ba6f3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2c57511-2e36-5133-bef3-7c7abf9f86dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-130104" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4xy+5{y}^{2}$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-4xy+5{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-4xy+5{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2x-4y)\\,i+(10y-4x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1) & =(2(-1)-4(-1))\\,i+(10(-1)-4(-1))\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨2,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),\\,y-(-1)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ee15d9b-2136-5535-95e9-65ca83b6e585" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365974} {:answer/id #uuid "4f99fbb7-b4a8-54b9-8934-23c7cef9e17d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365975} {:answer/id #uuid "452e3189-e023-5ff6-a8ac-b65cf2da5f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365976} {:answer/id #uuid "a35bd947-2863-5bdc-bbe2-ca2ddda89560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365977} {:answer/id #uuid "8180d1b4-d77a-5c86-994f-d8f6011242ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ee15d9b-2136-5535-95e9-65ca83b6e585" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365974} :answer-field/id #uuid "c431f4c8-fa06-53a4-8a34-6752a1ae7b03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c66f4ab-9b40-5d63-8243-7bb74991d0fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-130198" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)={f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)i-{f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)j \\\\  & =(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)j \\\\ t(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))j \\\\  & =i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $i-j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e9a0c2d-7c7b-5915-aabd-f66d3fadb6a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-e\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365981} {:answer/id #uuid "637d3258-d6d0-58ac-9935-850f46f2c755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365982} {:answer/id #uuid "adbad5ec-bc4f-5e66-8a72-5189d2261216" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365983} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc1939b-6b3a-5ae2-b8c5-f5b6b0b842ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365984} {:answer/id #uuid "9d3cfce2-3e55-57d5-b87a-6693662432ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+{e}^{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e9a0c2d-7c7b-5915-aabd-f66d3fadb6a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-e\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365981} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3009f29-c8c4-5804-b8aa-be20f0f1affd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e739b928-36f7-5211-98da-6e1454b4faf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-130200" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}=e-y.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1} & =e-y \\\\ x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=e.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,0)={f}_{y}(1,0)i-{f}_{x}(1,0)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y)j \\\\  & =(2xy{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+1)i-{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\ t(1,0) & =(2(1)(0){e}^{0+1}+1)i-{e}^{0+1}\\,j \\\\  & =i-e\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $i-e\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "848c573c-0c60-56a2-8200-5d76c9e9ab96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365988} {:answer/id #uuid "dd2edb55-5d52-5930-82fb-d3fb343230c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365989} {:answer/id #uuid "b55c4204-3245-5c5f-85dd-fe7c33e14ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365990} {:answer/id #uuid "868b77ce-cd12-5c2c-9573-776fccf736fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365991} {:answer/id #uuid "362ae0ad-93ac-56ba-9aef-927bd36b2e4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0)$ and $(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd2edb55-5d52-5930-82fb-d3fb343230c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ and $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365989} :answer-field/id #uuid "0586975f-4dc4-591c-bc01-6d8d84462534" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6851bbc7-80d6-5dc9-8a00-f559624fa12f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130217" :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2y-2x$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $-i+j?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-2)\\,i-(2x+2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $-i+j,$ we must have $t\\cdot (-i+j)=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot (-i+j) & =0 \\\\ ((2y-2)\\,i-(2x+2)\\,j)\\cdot (-i+j) & =0 \\\\ -(2y-2)-(2x+2) & =0 \\\\ -2y-2x & =0 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-x)^{2} & =2(-x)-2x \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =-4x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ 2x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get $y=2,$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,2).$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=0,$ and the corresponding point is $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "479f22f8-a0db-57b0-b2d1-91061253cab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365995} {:answer/id #uuid "d41105d4-a62c-5ff7-92a9-75326acd3b16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365996} {:answer/id #uuid "376112f6-ef70-5160-9f8f-f12c1111d550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365997} {:answer/id #uuid "c80e0137-13b2-5c30-a598-7ee2c7100551" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365998} {:answer/id #uuid "045fff26-8251-59be-8f51-56d7960d4869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "045fff26-8251-59be-8f51-56d7960d4869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365999} :answer-field/id #uuid "587ce2f9-f1b8-54d6-a699-faadf1247009" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c165c2b4-262b-5650-898d-47f3142c7c18" :question/math-academy-id "q-130223" :question/problem "At which point on the curve ${x}^{2}y=-8x-4$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =-8x-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}y+8x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+8x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-4.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+8x)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+8x)\\,j \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\,i-(2xy+8)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j,$ we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}\\,i-(2xy+8)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -(2xy+8) & =0 \\\\ xy+4 & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{4}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{4}{x}$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =-8x-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}(-\\frac{4}{x}) & =-8x-4 \\\\ -4x & =-8x-4 \\\\ 4x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $x=-1,$ we get $y=4,$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47104ed4-9649-51bb-b2a8-c666055b8c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366002} {:answer/id #uuid "9750f6df-b651-5d7d-b52d-4296485d6e02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2e-8)\\,i+(\\frac{e}{2}-8)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366003} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ec8b8b-fdac-5cbd-9b1f-e2056b05533b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8-2e)\\,i+(\\frac{8}{e}-2)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366004} {:answer/id #uuid "0fff4ab8-f5e6-549b-b371-ef71489ef400" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-4e)\\,i+(\\frac{2}{e}-8)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366005} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0232b1-b7ae-53d0-b628-061da369b4cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2-8e)\\,i+(2-8e)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47104ed4-9649-51bb-b2a8-c666055b8c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366002} :answer-field/id #uuid "25cc7d8c-201f-532a-b963-6e9e7da0546e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16105751-e8c9-525d-8bb4-60be36248f60" :question/math-academy-id "q-37516" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=8xy+2-8e.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =8xy+2-8e \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy & =2-8e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=2-8e.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,e)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,e)={f}_{y}(1,e)\\,i-{f}_{x}(1,e)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x}{y}-8x)i-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8y)\\,j \\\\ t(1,e) & =(\\frac{2(1)}{e}-8(1))i-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-8(e))\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ae7e9c8-60e9-5f5e-afbf-1de6bb01bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Line to a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15067bac-5f50-5a66-9f78-d960be749231" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366009} {:answer/id #uuid "ea833ddb-10ba-5e0d-a21e-fda6784f8ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨15,10⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366010} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee58423-b275-534a-a420-03c0678a3d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨10,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366011} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a4a4da-c2ce-53b9-abcd-25d20cc32988" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-10,15⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366012} {:answer/id #uuid "4e33074e-7336-5091-b56c-2ac8e2b4d9f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5,10⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ee58423-b275-534a-a420-03c0678a3d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨10,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366011} :answer-field/id #uuid "096cf923-1846-56b4-9079-05fc47b71dc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7975c9aa-052b-5a25-9627-4a7d8aff7ed9" :question/math-academy-id "q-72371" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x{y}^{2}+y)\\,i+(2y{x}^{2}+x)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,2) & =(2(1)(2)^{2}+(2))\\,i+(2(2)(1)^{2}+(1))\\,j \\\\  & =10\\,i+5\\,j \\\\  & =⟨10,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,2)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨10,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ae7e9c8-60e9-5f5e-afbf-1de6bb01bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Line to a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b19fa08-b3d4-58bf-8b6a-6d0a921a167c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366016} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb58c24-f9de-5884-864d-bda87a6da32b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366017} {:answer/id #uuid "bb86f54d-3dfc-5df7-acc6-5c7099191532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-5,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366018} {:answer/id #uuid "84e85b34-e2ca-50b0-b18f-081553e7f467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366019} {:answer/id #uuid "e1dc6ab9-cfb6-5369-8e57-51391b3bb68e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1dc6ab9-cfb6-5369-8e57-51391b3bb68e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366020} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dd633ae-c063-5e27-a4cc-b4f697474c1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85c3a288-e595-578f-a534-db75ed9d872f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72377" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=2x{y}^{2}+xy+2x$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}+xy+2x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}+xy+2x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2{y}^{2}+y+2)\\,i+(4xy+x)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1) & =(2(1)^{2}+(1)+2)\\,i+(4(1)(1)+(1))\\,j \\\\  & =5\\,i+5\\,j \\\\  & =⟨5,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨5,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d701a25-9b1d-59e1-87c6-edee8a104153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366023} {:answer/id #uuid "b43bbefb-fb27-5318-b9f1-b71cbba3cb66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,i+6\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366024} {:answer/id #uuid "e28ecaba-deab-587e-a644-9bf0c621da2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+6\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366025} {:answer/id #uuid "b2582f4b-c1cd-53d0-806d-fe3486746ac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366026} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbdaf61-e1c9-57f0-b895-ab7d36ec509f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-6\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b43bbefb-fb27-5318-b9f1-b71cbba3cb66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,i+6\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366024} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ed1e1e0-70f1-5784-bb56-8cad9427026d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96e37626-e43e-531a-94f2-e33ce2598b5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-72400" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2xy+9.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2xy+9 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,2)={f}_{y}(-1,2)\\,i-{f}_{x}(-1,2)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-2x)\\,i-(2x-2y)\\,j \\\\ t(-1,2) & =(2(2)-2(-1))\\,i-(2(-1)-2(2))\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i+6\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $6\\,i+6\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebc980b5-b7aa-5431-9aa2-b29be3a530b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366030} {:answer/id #uuid "ba79566f-77e7-5fa4-bad8-c38119bf080e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-2,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366031} {:answer/id #uuid "56595132-b849-588a-aa5a-42a19c3c9886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0)$  and $(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366032} {:answer/id #uuid "edc63ae4-4757-5727-b3cb-80691563851b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366033} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe198f9-7bb5-5597-a520-88af09679064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(2,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba79566f-77e7-5fa4-bad8-c38119bf080e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-2,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366031} :answer-field/id #uuid "03179b55-c711-5e80-80a8-9df2a2adf187" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d4b6633-13b7-5d79-8080-f17cf9d74729" :question/math-academy-id "q-72410" :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}+2xy={x}^{2}+8$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2xy & ={x}^{2}+8 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=8.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2})j \\\\  & =2(y+x)\\,i-2(y-x)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ (2(y+x)\\,i-2(y-x)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -2(y-x) & =0 \\\\ y & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x)^{2}+2x(x)-{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=2$, we get $y=2$ and the corresponding point is $(2,2).$\n\n- If $x=-2$, we get $y=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c049c83c-9c86-5199-9040-52a839df1f85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,2)$ and $(-1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366037} {:answer/id #uuid "34235320-c4ca-5a03-b1ed-5f200c4e9bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366038} {:answer/id #uuid "df2007b9-1a30-5401-ad2c-9006c5f85634" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366039} {:answer/id #uuid "1902e698-563a-50d0-a405-7352e7441046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2)$ and $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366040} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8c4d42-c631-5685-90ac-e8275378eb9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,3)$ and $(3,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df2007b9-1a30-5401-ad2c-9006c5f85634" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366039} :answer-field/id #uuid "22616d4a-e2cf-5ea1-81f5-909e46e06881" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "49dc4b38-854b-53b5-ba44-1bf47c27ef1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-72412" :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}-4xy=-5{x}^{2}+1$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4xy & =-5{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2})j \\\\  & =2(y-2x)\\,i-(10x-4y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i$, we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ (2(y-2x)\\,i-(10x-4y)\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 2(y-2x) & =0 \\\\ y & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=2x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (2x)^{2}-4x(2x)+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=1$, we get $y=2$ and the corresponding point is $(1,2).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b523b5a0-67a5-5529-b14e-e88a0012617d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and  $(-4,4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366044} {:answer/id #uuid "a95cd1c3-ed25-50ea-8d93-755165c5d272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and  $(4,-4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366045} {:answer/id #uuid "cce0eccd-6d56-5372-ad17-370eda66d942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and  $(-2,4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366046} {:answer/id #uuid "dff55780-bef3-5460-b3f6-e4bc5fd3fddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,4)$ and  $(-4,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366047} {:answer/id #uuid "9e533125-7549-5a97-939b-5d50c7b811ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b523b5a0-67a5-5529-b14e-e88a0012617d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and  $(-4,4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366044} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9ddc5cd-e881-5d2a-95bb-6d203811d244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8952532d-36c9-5aa4-b168-c4c8dbeea7fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-72440" :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${x}^{2}+2xy=-5y+4$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy & =-5y+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2xy+5y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+2xy+5y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy+5y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy+5y)j \\\\  & =(2x+5)\\,i-(2x+2y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ((2x+5)\\,i-(2x+2y)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -(2x+2y) & =0 \\\\ x & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-y$ into the equation of our curve gives:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy+5y & =4 \\\\ (-y)^{2}+2(-y)y+5y & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+5y & =4 \\\\ -{y}^{2}+5y-4 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-5y+4 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-4) & =0 \\\\ y & =1\\,\\text{or}\\,y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $y=1$, we get $x=-1$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,1).$\n\n- If $y=4$, we get $x=-4$ and the corresponding point is $(-4,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30e97871-9f71-51cb-b7a6-a6900f0de48a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366051} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d5964b-9b27-59c8-bbbf-92c6e4ae0ecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366052} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0b8e54-9f88-5f9c-8765-1e8c685e24ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366053} {:answer/id #uuid "11c86f7d-2a1f-56b6-90ba-b7a84a1486e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366054} {:answer/id #uuid "e531895d-fed2-5e76-bd9e-df888ca8a2dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,i-5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11c86f7d-2a1f-56b6-90ba-b7a84a1486e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366054} :answer-field/id #uuid "a277a9e0-eeb9-5f7a-b18d-fa5624ee0a74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8caa22b5-e4df-56be-9965-2e83cfebc7d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-72463" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy=1-3x.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy & =1-3x \\\\ 2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,1)={f}_{y}(1,1)i-{f}_{x}(1,1)j\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x)j \\\\  & =(4y{x}^{3}-4x)i-(6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4y+3)j \\\\ t(1,1) & =(4(1)(1)^{3}-4(1))i-(6(1)^{2}(1)^{2}-4(1)+3)j \\\\  & =-5\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-5\\,j.$"}]}
